===Glyph origin===
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|豆|頁|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s|t2=head}}.

===Etymology===
Replaced earlier {{ltc-l|首|head|id=1}} due to homophony with {{m-lite|ltc|sc=Hant|手||tr=ɕɨuX|hand}} ({{zh-ref|Sagart, 1999}}).

Several etymologies have been proposed:
* From {{inh-lite|zh|sit-pro|*tV-pu||head}} or its allofam {{m-lite|sit-pro|*du||head}}; compare {{cog-lite|bo|sc=Tibt|དབུ||tr=dbu|head}}, {{cog-lite|my|sc=Mymr|ဦး||tr=u:|head}}, {{cog-lite|ii|sc=Yiii|ꀑ||tr=o|head}}, {{cog-lite|tbq-lol-pro|*ʔ-du²||head}} ({{zh-ref|Benedict, 1972}}; {{zh-ref|Schuessler, 2007}}; STEDT; {{zh-ref|Starostin}}).
* {{contr|zh}} of {{och-l|髑髏|gloss=skull}} ({{zh-ref|Wu, 2006}}).
* From {{der-lite|zh|mkh-pro|*dulʔ|*duul()|to carry on one's head}}, whence {{cog-lite|km|sc=Khmr|ទូល|tr=tul}}; this is phonologically plausible since final consonants are often lost after a long vowel in loanwords ({{zh-ref|Schuessler, 2007}}).
* A variant of {{och-l|豆|gloss=a kind of vessel}}, as skulls could have been used as drinking recipients; compare {{cog-lite|fr|tête||head}} from {{cog-lite|la|testa||pot; jug}} and {{cog-lite|de|Kopf||head}} from {{cog-lite|gmh|kopf||drinking vessel}} ({{zh-ref|Maréchal, 1994}}; {{zh-ref|Sagart, 1999}}). However, {{zh-ref|Schuessler (2007)}} notes that {{m-lite|och|sc=Hant|豆|tr=*doːs}} seems like a tone C ({{l-lite|zh|sc=Hani|去聲}}) derivation from {{m-lite|och|sc=Hant|頭|tr=*doː}}, which may have originally meant “skull”.
* {{zh-ref|Baxter and Sagart (2014)}} reconstructs the Old Chinese with a ''*m-'' prefix for human body parts, deriving it from {{m-lite|och|sc=Hant|兜||tr=*tˤo|helmet; hood}}.

