===Etymology 1===
{{etymid|ja|atama}}
{{ja-kanjitab|あたま|yomi=k}}
{{wp|ja:}}
{{wp|Head}}

Probably from {{inh-lite|ja|ojp|sort=あたま|-}}.  First cited in the ten-volume ''{{w|Wamyō Ruijushō}}'' of 934 {{CE}}.<ref name="KDJ">{{R:Kokugo Dai Jiten}}</ref>

{{unknown|ja|sort=あたま}} derivation. Theories include:
* Compound of {{ja-com|天|あ|玉|たま|t1=[[heaven]], [[sky]]|t2=[[ball]]}}. This is problematic phonetically, as {{m|ja|天}} appears historically as ''ama'' or ''ame'', not as just ''a''.
* Compound of {{ja-com|当て|あて|pos1={{ja-ryk|当てる|あてる|to [[hit]]|nodot=y}}|間|ま|t1=[[put]]ting something in [[contact]]|t2=[[space]], [[gap]]}}, referring to the head as a [[moxibustion]] point.  This is also problematic phonetically, as the shift from ''ate'' to ''ata'' necessitates a change in meaning of the underlying verb.  This is also problematic semantically, as the term {{ja-r|間|ま}} term refers more specifically to a [[gap]] or [[space]].
* Compound of {{ja-com|貴|あて|間|ま|t1=[[noble]]|t2=space}}, referring to the most important part of the body.  However, the use of {{ja-r|間|ま}} in this way is again problematic.  Moreover, {{ja-r|貴|あて|noble}} appears in ''{{w|The Tale of the Bamboo Cutter}}'' dating to the 900s CE, while {{ja-r|頭|あたま}} with a sense of [[fontanelle]] appears in the ''{{w|Wamyō Ruijushō}}'' dated 938 CE, leaving insufficient time for either the semantic or phonetic drift required.<ref name="KDJ">{{R:Kokugo Dai Jiten}}</ref><ref name="DJR">{{R:Daijirin}}</ref>

Historically, this term first appears with a sense of [[fontanelle]], in reference to the soft place on the top of an infant's head where the bones of the skull have not yet fused.  As such, a more likely derivation might be as a compound of {{ja-com|当た|あた|t1=[[not quite]] [[touch]]ing, [[not quite]] in [[contact]]|pos1={{ja-r|未%然%形|み%ぜん%けい|[[irrealis]] form}} of classical verb {{ja-r|当つ|あつ}} and root of modern {{ja-r|当たる|あたる|to [[touch]], to come into [[contact]]}}|間|ま|t2=[[space]], [[gap]], [[opening]]}}.

