===Etymology 5===
{{etymid|ja|tsuburi}}
{{ja-kanjitab|つぶり|yomi=irr}}

Cognate with {{m-lite|ja|sc=Jpan|粒|tr=tsubu||round thing|pos=such as a grain}}, {{m-lite|ja|sc=Jpan|禿ぶ|tr=tsubu||to go bald|pos=from the idea of one's head becoming ''round''}}, {{m-lite|ja|sc=Jpan|円ら|tr=tsubura|pos=adjective||round}}, {{m-lite|ja|sc=Jpan|潰れる|tr=tsubureru||to become rounded, as from wear and tear, or from crushing}}.<ref name="KDJ">{{R:Kokugo Dai Jiten}}</ref>

Some sources<ref name="DJR">{{R:Daijirin}}</ref> derive this as a shift from {{m-lite|ja|sc=Jpan|円ら|tr=tsubura|pos=adjective||round}}.  However, the phonology and semantics for this do not fit (changing {{IPAchar-lite|/a/}} to {{IPAchar-lite|/i/}}, and repurposing the adjectivizing suffix {{m-lite|ja|sc=Jpan|ら}} to instead form a noun).  The modern verb ''tsubureru'' had the form ''tsuburu'' in older stages of the language.  The ''tsuburi'' reading for {{lang-lite|ja|sc=Jpan|頭}} may more likely represent a nominalization derived from this older verb, following normal patterns for creating nouns from verbs.

Compare {{cog-lite|ryu|sc=Jpan|ちぶる||tr=chiburu|[[head]]}}.

